# contrib/action.yml
# Composite action used BY A DIVISION'S OWN REPOSITORY to request a perimeter change.
#
# The division's repository never applies anything and needs NO permission in Access Context Manager —
# and, since the contract moved to a release asset, NO identity in Google Cloud at all. It needs exactly
# one thing: a GitHub App token on THIS repository. See the `app-token` input for what that token really
# has to be able to do — it is not what the phrase "it only opens a pull request" suggests.
#
# WHY the contract is fetched from a release and not from `gs://…`: reading the bucket copy requires the
# division's repository to federate an identity into GCP and hold a grant on the contract prefix. Thirty
# divisions meant thirty grants — issued so that someone could read 4 KB of JSON. The bucket copy still
# exists for machine consumers outside GitHub; it is simply no longer on this path. Both copies are written
# by the SAME apply step (see apply.yml), so there is no "which one is current" question to ask.
#
# WHY not let them apply: Access Context Manager rewrites the whole org-level policy on every change and
# guards it with an eTag. Parallel appliers do NOT lose rules silently — the loser fails loudly with
# `Error 400: The eTag provided ... does not match` (measured: ~80-100% of time-overlapping runs). That is not
# reassuring: with two mutators every other merge randomly fails and needs a retry, and each new division
# raises the collision rate. On top of that, `servicePerimeters.update` cannot be scoped below the
# organization, so every such repository would get the right to change the boundary for the entire company.
#
# WHY the contract + gates bundle instead of a submodule: a submodule hands over the WHOLE perimeter
# repository — including every division's service accounts (perimeter/members/) and your corporate IP ranges
# (perimeter/access-levels/). Validating one file of your own needs neither.
#
# Usage in the division's repository:
#
#   - uses: actions/checkout@11d5960a326750d5838078e36cf38b85af677262 # v4.4.0
#   - uses: ORG/gcp-vpc-sc/contrib@main
#     with:
#       member-file: vpc-sc/prj-example-vertex-prod.yaml
#       perimeter-repo: ORG/gcp-vpc-sc
#       app-token: ${{ steps.app.outputs.token }}

name: Request VPC-SC perimeter change
description: Validate a member declaration against the published contract, then ask the perimeter repository to open a PR.

inputs:
  member-file:
    description: Path to the member YAML in THIS repository.
    required: true
  perimeter-repo:
    description: ORG/REPO of the perimeter repository.
    required: true
  app-token:
    description: >
      Token of a GitHub App installed on both repositories, with `contents: write` on the perimeter
      repository — and nothing else. MEASURED, because the intuitive answer is wrong in both directions:
      the last step here calls `POST /repos/{owner}/{repo}/dispatches`, which needs Contents WRITE
      (`contents: read` + `pull-requests: write` returns HTTP 403 "Resource not accessible by
      integration"), while `pull_requests` is not needed at all — the pull request is opened on the other
      side by `external-intake.yml`, with the perimeter repository's own GITHUB_TOKEN. Contents write is
      broader than "open a pull request"; what actually bounds this channel is `perimeter/contributors.yaml`
      living on the other side, the payload being data rather than authorization, and apply running only
      from the default branch. A narrower variant exists (`workflow_dispatch` needs `actions: write`, which
      grants no code write) and costs a change on both sides of the channel. GITHUB_TOKEN will NOT work
      either way: it is scoped to the repository it was issued for.
    required: true
  gates-version:
    description: >
      Release tag of the gates bundle, or `latest`. Pin it if you want reproducible validation; use `latest`
      to see new rules the moment the platform ships them.
    required: false
    default: latest

runs:
  using: composite
  steps:
    - name: install tooling
      shell: bash
      run: |
        pip install --quiet pyyaml check-jsonschema
        curl -sSfL -o /tmp/conftest.tar.gz \
          https://github.com/open-policy-agent/conftest/releases/download/v0.56.0/conftest_0.56.0_Linux_x86_64.tar.gz
        tar -xzf /tmp/conftest.tar.gz -C /tmp conftest
        sudo mv /tmp/conftest /usr/local/bin/

    # Gates = RULES only (schemas + rego). No member data, no IP ranges — see publish-gates.yml.
    - name: fetch the gates bundle
      shell: bash
      env:
        GH_TOKEN: ${{ inputs.app-token }}
      run: |
        if [ "${{ inputs.gates-version }}" = "latest" ]; then
          gh release download --repo "${{ inputs.perimeter-repo }}" --pattern 'gates.tar.gz' --clobber
        else
          gh release download "${{ inputs.gates-version }}" --repo "${{ inputs.perimeter-repo }}" \
            --pattern 'gates.tar.gz' --clobber
        fi
        tar -xzf gates.tar.gz

    # Contract = the list of options you may choose from (profiles, access level names, your own projects).
    #
    # The `contract` release tag is deliberately NOT an input and deliberately NOT pinnable. Gates are RULES,
    # so pinning them buys reproducible validation; the contract is a snapshot of STATE, so a pinned copy
    # means validating against profiles and access levels that may no longer exist — a green run that lies.
    # The perimeter repository moves this tag on every apply, exactly like `Cache-Control: no-store` on the
    # bucket copy.
    - name: fetch the contract
      shell: bash
      env:
        GH_TOKEN: ${{ inputs.app-token }}
      run: |
        gh release download contract --repo "${{ inputs.perimeter-repo }}" \
          --pattern 'contract.json' --clobber
        echo "contract from $(python3 -c 'import json;print(json.load(open("contract.json"))["schema_version"])') schema, \
        $(python3 -c 'import json;print(len(json.load(open("contract.json"))["profiles"]))') profiles available"

    # Validating HERE is the point: the team sees the error in its own pipeline, in seconds, instead of in
    # a rejected PR in someone else's repository.
    - name: validate the declaration
      shell: bash
      run: |
        bash ./gates/validate-local.sh \
          --member "${{ inputs.member-file }}" \
          --gates ./gates \
          --contract ./contract.json

    # change_ref is built by the ACTION, not read from the file — a self-declared reference proves nothing,
    # and the perimeter repository cross-checks it against the repository that actually sent the dispatch.
    - name: request the change
      shell: bash
      env:
        GH_TOKEN: ${{ inputs.app-token }}
      run: |
        change_ref="pr:${GITHUB_REPOSITORY}#${{ github.event.pull_request.number || github.run_number }}"
        python3 -c "
        import json, os, yaml
        m = yaml.safe_load(open('${{ inputs.member-file }}'))
        m['change_ref'] = '$change_ref'
        print(json.dumps({'event_type': 'vpc-sc-external', 'client_payload': {
            'member': m, 'source_repository': os.environ['GITHUB_REPOSITORY'],
            'source_sha': os.environ['GITHUB_SHA']}}))
        " | gh api --method POST "repos/${{ inputs.perimeter-repo }}/dispatches" --input -
        echo "Zgłoszenie wysłane jako $change_ref — PR powstanie w ${{ inputs.perimeter-repo }}."
